<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scipio Aemilianus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 185–129 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Scipio Aemilianus was a Roman general and statesman who commanded the final siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a>. In 146 BCE he captured and destroyed the city. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> treat this as the end of Carthage as a state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus.jpg" alt="Scipio Aemilianus" width="277" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_statue_of_a_Hellenistic_prince,_1st_half_of_2nd_century_BC,_found_on_the_Quirinal_in_Rome,_Palazzo_Massimo_alle_Terme,_Rome_(31479801364).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Scipio Aemilianus</b> finally surrounded the city with a ring of camps and walls and starved it into surrender in 133 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On his death in 148 BCE <b>Scipio Aemilianus</b>, acting for Rome, divided royal functions among his three legitimate sons: Micipsa held Cirta and the government, Gulussa the army and Mastanabal justice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Punic War of 149–146 BCE was a siege of a city that posed little real threat, ending when <b>Scipio Aemilianus</b>’s forces stormed Carthage after six days of street fighting and razed it, tradition holding that the city burned for seventeen days, while an estimated 50,000 survivors were sold…</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 134 BCE <b>Scipio Aemilianus</b> surrounded Numantia with walls and starved it; Retogenes broke out to seek aid, but the town fell in 133 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/arevaci">Arevaci</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-149" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Scipio Aemilianus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-149">What was here in 149 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;O Polybius, it is a grand thing, but, I know not how, I feel a terror and dread, lest some one should one day give the same order about my own native city.&#8221;</p><footer>Polybius, Histories, book 38 &#183; tr. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh, 1889 &#183; as he gave the order to burn Carthage, 146 BCE; Polybius, who stood beside him, records it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44126/pg44126-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
